.Fl \-help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
pocketparse is a small, embeddable C library that compiles grammar data
into running parsers at runtime.
It is a pocket knife for C programs that
need parsing without adopting a generator\-heavy toolchain, a scripting
runtime, libc, malloc, a filesystem, or OS assumptions.
.Pp
The core promise is simple: give pocketparse grammar bytes and input
bytes, and it gives back a streaming parser, a concrete syntax tree, and
structured diagnostics.
The library itself is freestanding — no libc, no
IO, no allocation outside a host\-granted memory pool.
This program is the
hosted command\-line
shell around that library: it reads files, renders diagnostics, and prints.
.Pp
The native grammar surface is Scheme\-shaped data, the \fB.scm\fR format.
It is
data, not executable Scheme: lists, symbols, keyword symbols ending in a
colon, strings, integers, byte and hex literals, and line comments.
A
grammar declares tokens, extras, and rules built from the combinators
\fBseq\fR, \fBchoice\fR, \fBrepeat\fR, \fBoptional\fR, and \fBfield\fR.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm check Ar GRAMMAR Oo Fl \-param Cm NAME[=VALUE Oc Oc
Read and validate a grammar without parsing any input.
Report malformed
grammar as a diagnostic on standard error and exit non\-zero; a valid
grammar exits zero.
.It Cm run Ar GRAMMAR Fl \-input Ar FILE Oo Fl \-dump-tree Oc Oo Fl \-param Cm NAME[=VALUE Oc Oc
Compile a grammar, parse the bytes of the input file against it, and,
with \-\-dump\-tree, print the resulting concrete syntax tree to standard
output \-\- one node per line, indented by depth, each line ending with the
node's span as a byte range and a 0\-based [line, column] range, tree\-sitter
style.
Diagnostics are rendered to standard error.
\-\-param sets a compile\-time grammar parameter, the value the (when NAME:
X) combinator gates a production on: a bare \-\-param NAME sets it truthy,
\-\-param NAME=0 clears it.
The flag may be repeated to set several
parameters.
A grammar that extends a base with (extends NAME) and gates
productions on parameters compiles differently for each set of \-\-param
values.
When GRAMMAR names a directory (or a package.scm file), it is read as a
grammar package: the package.scm manifest is parsed, its params: are
bound as compile\-time parameters, and the sibling grammar.scm is compiled
with those parameters in scope.
A \-\-param on the command line overrides a
manifest default. check accepts a package the same way.
A GRAMMAR may also be a bare package name, such as std.json.
When no
filesystem search path resolves the name, pocketparse falls back to the
registry of standard grammars embedded in the binary, so a standard
grammar parses with no package on disk.
A filesystem package of the same
name found first shadows the embedded copy.
The same fallback applies to a
base named by (extends NAME) or a module named by (import NAME): the
resolver tries the filesystem first and, failing that, looks the name up in
the embedded registry, so an embedded package such as std.jsonc resolves
its (extends std.json) base with no files at all.
.It Cm linearize Ar GRAMMAR Fl \-input Ar FILE Oo Fl \-param Cm NAME[=VALUE Oc Oc
Compile a grammar, parse the input file against it, and print the parse
tree's linearization to standard output: one ENTER/LEAF/EXIT token per line.
An internal node prints as \fBenter KIND\fR then, after its children, \fBexit KIND\fR;
a leaf (token) node prints as \fBleaf KIND TEXT\fR.
This is the flattened stream a
query parses — a query matches a pattern by parsing this token stream — and the
command is the debugging view of it.
GRAMMAR is resolved exactly as for run.
.It Cm query Ar GRAMMAR Ar QUERY Fl \-input Ar FILE Oo Fl \-param Cm NAME[=VALUE Oc Oc
Compile a grammar, parse the input file against it, then run the QUERY file
(a \fB.scm\fR query) over the resulting tree and print one line per capture as
\fB@NAME START\-END TEXT\fR.
A query is a parser over the linearized tree: a
pattern such as \fB(Item word: (Word) @w)\fR matches by parsing the ENTER/LEAF/EXIT
stream the linearize command prints.
A pattern matches at any depth (descendant
matching skips one balanced subtree); a \fB.\fR anchor between two children forbids
that skip, requiring them adjacent; \fB_\fR is the wildcard node; a \fBfield:\fR keyword
binds a child through its field; \fB@name\fR captures the node it follows.
A
wrapping list pairs a pattern with predicates: \fB(#eq? @c "text")\fR keeps a match
only when the capture's bytes equal the text, and \fB(#match? @c "regex")\fR only
when the engine's regex matches them.
Both predicates are built in and need no
host binding.
GRAMMAR is resolved exactly as for run.
.It Cm node-types Ar GRAMMAR Oo Fl \-param Cm NAME[=VALUE Oc Oc
Compile a grammar and print its node\-types schema to standard output as a
JSON document, the typed surface tools read.
The schema follows
tree\-sitter's node\-types.json shape: a JSON array of node entries, one per
named rule and named token.
Each entry carries its "type" name, a "named"
flag, and a "fields" object mapping each field name to the named types that
field may hold; a rule with unnamed positional children also carries a
"children" object.
A rule with no fields still emits an empty "fields"
object, so the key is always present.
GRAMMAR is resolved exactly as for
check and run, so a package directory or an embedded grammar name works too.
.It Cm init Ar DIR Oo Fl \-amalgamation|--lib Oc Oo Fl \-grammar Ar NAME Oc
Seed a new project in DIR.
In \-\-amalgamation mode, the default, it drops
the whole engine as two files — pocketparse.c and pocketparse.h —
alongside a starter grammar.scm,
a main.c that compiles that grammar at runtime and dumps the parse of
standard input, and a one\-line build.sh.
The seeded project then builds
with a single \fBcc main.c pocketparse.c \-o app\fR and needs no pocketparse
tooling on the path.
The embedded engine source is baked into the program
at build time, like the manual page and the grammar registry, so init needs
no install prefix and no network.
In \-\-lib mode it writes the same main.c
and grammar.scm but copies no engine source, leaving the project to link an
installed libpocketparse.
With \-\-grammar NAME the starter grammar is the
named standard grammar (for example std.json) instead of the built\-in hello
grammar.
.It Cm vendor Ar DIR Oo Fl \-amalgamation|--lib Oc
Drop or refresh just the engine source in an existing project DIR, writing
no scaffold.
In \-\-amalgamation mode it writes pocketparse.c and
pocketparse.h; in \-\-lib mode it writes only pocketparse.h, for a project
that links an installed libpocketparse.
.It Cm grammars
List the standard grammar packages embedded in the binary, one name per
line.
These are the grammars resolvable by name with no filesystem present
(for example std.json); the registry is baked into the program at build
time, like the manual page.
.It Cm man
Print this manual page, in mdoc form, to standard output.
The page is
embedded in the program at build time, so it is available with no
filesystem lookup.
.It Cm version
Print the program name and version, then exit zero.
.It Fl \-help
Print a short usage summary and exit zero.
Running pocketparse with no
arguments prints the same usage summary.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
pocketparse exits 0 on success, and non\-zero when a grammar fails to
validate, an input fails to parse, or the command line is malformed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Validate a grammar:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pocketparse check examples/sexpr/sexpr.scm
.Ed
.Pp
Parse an input file and dump its tree:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pocketparse run examples/sexpr/sexpr.scm \-\-input sample.txt \-\-dump\-tree
.Ed
.Pp
Read this manual:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pocketparse man | mandoc
.Ed
